WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.830
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:10.059
The promise of virtual reality, VR is indeed
truly incredible.

00:00:10.059 --> 00:00:15.340
If one day it comes to fruition, doctors could
be trained to perform surgery in a virtual

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:21.380
environment, we could train better pilots
with better flight simulators, expose astronauts

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:25.339
to virtual zero-gravity simulations, you name
it.

00:00:25.339 --> 00:00:31.280
This previous work uses a learning-based algorithm
to teach a head-mounted camera to tell the

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.340
orientation of our hands at all times.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.770
Okay, so what can we do with this?

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:38.770
A great deal.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:44.840
For instance, we can type on a virtual keyboard,
or implement all kinds of virtual user interfaces

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.020
that we can interact with.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.980
We can also organize imaginary boxes, and
of course, we can’t leave out the the Two

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:57.960
Minute Papers favorite, going into a physics
simulation and playing with it with our own

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:58.960
hands.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.420
But of course, not everything is perfect here,
however.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:03.420
Look.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.479
Hand-hand interactions don’t work so well,
so folks who prefer virtual reality applications

00:01:08.479 --> 00:01:11.259
that include washing our hands should look
elsewhere.

00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:17.420
And in this series, we often say “one more
paper down the line, and it will be significantly

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:18.420
better”.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:23.479
So now, here is the moment of truth, let’s
see that one more paper down the line.

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:31.020
Let’s go in guns blazing and give it examples
with challenging hand-hand interactions, deformations,

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.500
lots of self-contact and self-occlusion.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.719
Take a look at this footage.

00:01:36.719 --> 00:01:41.130
This seems like a nightmare for any hand reconstruction
algorithm.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.029
Who the heck can solve this?

00:01:43.029 --> 00:01:49.429
And, look, interestingly, they also recorded
the hand model with gloves on.

00:01:49.429 --> 00:01:51.490
How curious!

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:56.759
And now, hold on to your papers because these
are not gloves…no, no, no.

00:01:56.759 --> 00:02:01.779
What you see here is the reconstruction of
the hand model by a new algorithm.

00:02:01.779 --> 00:02:02.840
Look.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.229
It can deal with all of these rapid hand motions.

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:10.970
And what’s more, it also works on this challenging
hand massage scene.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.420
Look at all those beautiful details!

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:19.200
It not only fits like a glove here too, but
I see creases, folds, and deformations too

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.540
- this reconstruction is truly out of this
world.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:29.060
To be able to do this, the algorithm has to
output triangle meshes that typically contain

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.690
over a hundred thousand faces.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:35.570
Please remember this as we will talk about
it later.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:40.310
And now, let’s see how it does all this
magic, because there is plenty of magic under

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:41.310
the hood.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:46.270
Let’s look at the five ingredients that
are paramount to getting an output of this

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:47.270
quality.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:50.160
Ingredient number one is the physics term.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.760
Without it, we can’t even dream of tracking
self-occlusion and contact properly.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:03:00.630
Two, since there are plenty of deformations
going on in the input footage, the deformation

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.920
term accounts for that.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.120
It makes a huge difference in the reconstruction
of the thumb here.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.830
And if you think “wow, that is horrific”,
then you’ll need to hold on to your papers

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:17.140
for the next one, which is…three…the geometric
consistency term.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:19.560
This one is not for the faint of the heart.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.860
You have been warned.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:23.410
Are you ready?

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.830
Let’s go.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:27.040
Yikes!

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:33.010
A piece of advice, if you decide to implement
this technique, make sure to include this

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:38.680
geometric consistency term so no one has to
see this footage ever again.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:39.840
Thank you.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:45.970
With the worst already behind us, let’s
proceed to ingredient number four, the photo-consistency

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:46.970
term.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:53.680
This ensures that fingernail tips don’t
end up sliding into the finger.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:59.000
And five, the collision term fixes problems
like this to make sure that the fingers don’t

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.710
penetrate each other.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:07.760
And this is an excellent paper, so in the
evaluation section, these terms are also tested

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:13.780
in isolation, and the authors tell us exactly
how much each of these ingredients contribute

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:15.330
to the solution.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:20.660
Now, these five ingredients are not cheap
in terms of computation time, and remember,

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:25.750
we also mentioned that many of these meshes
have several hundred thousand faces.

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:29.850
This means that this technique takes a very
long time to compute all this.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:35.880
It is not real time, not even close, for instance,
reconstructing the mesh for the hand massage

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:39.880
scene takes more than 10 minutes per frame.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:44.850
This means hours, or even days of computation
to accomplish this.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:49.580
Now the question naturally arises - is that
a problem?

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:52.300
No, not in the slightest.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:58.389
This is a zero to one paper, which means that
it takes a problem that was previously impossible,

00:04:58.389 --> 00:05:00.750
and now, it makes it possible.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.190
That is absolutely amazing.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:08.180
And as always, research is a process, and
this is an important stepping stone in this

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:09.180
process.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:14.730
I bet that two more good papers down the line,
and we will be getting these gloves interactively.

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:21.130
I am so happy about this solution, as it could
finally give us new ways interact with each

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:27.690
other in virtual spaces, add more realism
to digital characters, help us better understand

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:34.730
human-human interactions, and it may also
enable new applications in physical rehabilitation.

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:39.150
And these reconstructions indeed fit like
a glove.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:50.100
What a time 
to 

00:05:50.100 --> 00:06:25.290
be alive!

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:33.550
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

